      "status_notes": "CVE-2026-3479: pkgutil.get_data() did not reject absolute or parent-directory resource names, although its documentation said it did, so a caller passing an untrusted resource name could read files outside the package. Upstream has disputed the CVE: it reverted the input checks it first added (https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/bcdf231946b1da8bdfbab4c05539bb0cc964a1c7, reverted in https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/cf59bf76470f3d75ad47d80ffb8ce76b64b5e943) and changed the documentation of the 3.13 and later branches to say pkgutil.get_data() has the same security model as open(); the 3.12 branch received no change (https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/146121, https://mail.python.org/archives/list/security-announce@python.org/thread/WYLLVQOOCKGK73JM7Z7ZSNOJC4N7BAWY/). The CVE record is tagged disputed and lists every release before 3.13.13 as affected: https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-3479. The DHI Alpine 3.23 python-3.12 package carries a DHI backport of the reverted upstream check, which makes pkgutil.get_data() raise ValueError for an absolute resource name or one containing a .. component. Read the source of pkgutil.get_data() with the interpreter from every published build (3.12.12-r0 to 3.12.16-r4) on x86_64 and aarch64: 3.12.12-r0, 3.12.13-r0 and 3.12.13-r1 lack the check; 3.12.13-r2 is the first published build that has it and every later build carries it. The range ends at 3.12.13-r2 because it is the first published build with the check."
