      "status": "affected",
      "action_statement": "Update python-3.14 to 3.14.4-r2 or later.",
      "status_notes": "CVE-2026-3479: pkgutil.get_data() did not reject absolute or parent-directory resource names, although its documentation said it did, so a caller passing an untrusted resource name could read files outside the package. Upstream has disputed the CVE: it reverted the input checks it first added (https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/bcdf231946b1da8bdfbab4c05539bb0cc964a1c7, reverted in https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/cf59bf76470f3d75ad47d80ffb8ce76b64b5e943) and changed the documentation to say pkgutil.get_data() has the same security model as open(); the 3.14 branch received only that documentation change (https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/146121, https://mail.python.org/archives/list/security-announce@python.org/thread/WYLLVQOOCKGK73JM7Z7ZSNOJC4N7BAWY/). The CVE record is tagged disputed and lists 3.14.4 as the first unaffected 3.14 release on the strength of that documentation change; https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-3479. Compared Lib/pkgutil.py in every published build, 3.14.3-r0 to 3.14.10-r4, of the DHI Alpine 3.23 python-3.14 package on x86_64 and aarch64 with the files in the upstream CPython 3.14 releases. 3.14.3-r0 to 3.14.4-r1 lack the fix; 3.14.4-r2 is the first published build that has it and carries a DHI patch that adds the rejected check (a ValueError for an absolute resource name or one containing a .. component), and every later build carries it. 3.14.4-r1 ships the CPython 3.14.4 Lib/pkgutil.py unchanged, which upstream treats as unaffected; it is kept on the affected side here because it does not have the check."
    },
