      "status_notes": "CVE-2026-3479: pkgutil.get_data() did not reject absolute or parent-directory resource names, although its documentation said it did, so a caller passing an untrusted resource name could read files outside the package. Upstream has disputed the CVE: it reverted the input checks it first added (https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/bcdf231946b1da8bdfbab4c05539bb0cc964a1c7, reverted in https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/cf59bf76470f3d75ad47d80ffb8ce76b64b5e943) and changed the documentation of the 3.13 and later branches to say pkgutil.get_data() has the same security model as open(); the 3.12 branch received no change (https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/146121, https://mail.python.org/archives/list/security-announce@python.org/thread/WYLLVQOOCKGK73JM7Z7ZSNOJC4N7BAWY/). The CVE record is tagged disputed and lists every release before 3.13.13 as affected: https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-3479. The DHI Debian 13 python-3.12 packages carry a DHI backport of the reverted upstream check, which makes pkgutil.get_data() raise ValueError for an absolute resource name or one containing a .. component. Compared Lib/pkgutil.py in the standard library shipped by these packages (in libpython-3.12-minimal or libpython-3.12-stdlib, depending on the build) in every build published in the DHI Debian repository, 3.12.13-0 to 3.12.16-8, amd64 and arm64, with the upstream file, which is the same in every 3.12 release from 3.12.12 to 3.12.16 and has no such check: every published build, on both architectures, ships that upstream file plus exactly the check from commit bcdf2319. Every published build has it, so the range ends at the lowest published build, 3.12.13-0."
